**Read:** [Large Volume Inorganic Chemicals — Solids and Others Industry, BREF LVIC-S](../sources/Inorganic-Solids-and-Others-BREF.lingua#r_1M61GTSHDW8CPV4PNH48PTB4Y4), the whole titanium-dioxide and carbon-black chapters with generic product and environmental methods; [Production of Speciality Inorganic Chemicals, BREF SIC](../sources/Speciality-Inorganic-Chemicals-BREF.lingua#r_04G7NFYXE76QCB3DXCRJC1FSCP), the complete relevant specialty-pigment treatment; [Chemical Technology: From Principles to Products — Jess and Wasserscheid](../sources/Chemical-Technology-Jess-Wasserscheid.lingua#r_38DBBMW0BMR89K3R95V073YA41), the full titanium-dioxide example if using the authorized second edition.

Titanium-dioxide and carbon-black production illustrate why chemical identity alone is insufficient for industrial solids. Crystal form, particle/aggregate distribution, surface treatment, impurities, color or optical behavior, and application performance can distinguish useful grades.

For titanium dioxide, compare complete chloride and sulfate route descriptions through feed, intermediate chemistry, purification, product formation and residuals. For carbon black, distinguish controlled carbon-forming chemistry and product structure from indiscriminate combustion residue. Neither family is defined fully by a bulk elemental assay.

Trace gases, acids/salts, heat, water, particles and surface-treatment substances through the process. Finishing and packaging can change contamination or physical distribution. A claimed change in yield or energy must be compared at the same actual grade/function and supporting measurement conditions.

**Work:** reconstruct one complete detailed product chapter and a justified alternative route/grade using the primary technical methods. Independently close the target and consequential impurity accounts, compare utilities/residuals and explain the structural/surface observations linking production to application. Identify a further experiment capable of challenging the proposed causal explanation.

**Continue:** [Define carbon feedstocks by composition, origin, variability and the intended transformation](../organic/Carbon-feedstocks-molecular-mixtures-and-source-boundaries.lingua#r_0EB7JVAJMYYF2Z76CM4R0Z91PG).

